### Is there an already existing issue for this?

- [X] I have searched the existing issues

I searched the open and closed issues and found none. #5019 is about the same branch dropping samples after a clock change; it does not mention the leaked change. ros2/rmw_fastrtps#789 shows similar long-run /tf symptoms, but its proposed fix (#5859) changes `min_low_mark` clipping, which is different code.

### Expected behavior

A DataReader with KEEP_LAST history discards a sample that is older than every sample in its full instance, and returns that sample's change to the reader's pool.

### Current behavior

`DataReaderHistory::received_change_keep_last` returns `true` for that sample without adding it to the history or releasing it (v2.14.7, `src/cpp/fastdds/subscriber/history/DataReaderHistory.cpp:290-294`):

```cpp
            else
            {
                // Received change is older than oldest, and should be discarded
                return true;
            }
```

`StatefulReader::processDataMsg` releases the reserved change only when `change_received` returns `false` (`src/cpp/rtps/reader/StatefulReader.cpp:675-686`). So the change is never returned to the pool. Each such sample costs one entry, until `reserve_cache` fails at `StatefulReader.cpp:616` and the reader refuses every new sample while its history is full. With default QoS the pool holds `max_samples + 1` = 5001 entries.

The same branch is on the 2.14.x, 3.2.x and master branches today (master `DataReaderHistory.cpp:306-307`). The fragment-completion path in the same file (`completed_change_keep_last`, v2.14.7 line 827) has the same `return true`. We did not test whether it leaks too.

It happens whenever a keyless topic (or one instance) has more than one writer and the reader's history is full when a sample arrives stamped earlier than the instance's front sample. That is common on ROS 2 `/tf`, with many writers at different rates. In one 14-minute run of our ROS 2 Jazzy application, a `/tf` reader leaked 4901 entries (about 6 a second). It refused its first sample in the same millisecond that the leaked count reached 4901, which is 5001 minus the history depth of 100. We traced that with an instrumented 2.14.7 build that logs the pool's allocated and free counts and each hit of the branch above. The held count tracked the hit count throughout.

### Steps to reproduce

Self-contained reproducer (`leak_repro.cpp`, below):

- One participant, one keyless topic.
- Writers A and B, both reliable.
- One reliable KEEP_LAST(depth) reader with `resource_limits.max_samples = M`. It reads but never takes, so its history stays full.
- B fills the history. Each round, A writes one sample with `write_w_timestamp(now - 10 s)`, then B writes one sample stamped now.

```
$ ./leak_repro 5 20 30          # depth 5, max_samples 20
fill: B wrote 5, reader got 5
round  1: old A sample + fresh B sample 7 -> reader got 1, newest 7
...
round 15: old A sample + fresh B sample 35 -> reader got 1, newest 35
round 16: old A sample + fresh B sample 37 -> reader got 0, newest 35 (fresh sample missing)
...
round 30: old A sample + fresh B sample 65 -> reader got 0, newest 35 (fresh sample missing)
after 3 s: reader got 0 more, newest 35 of 65 written
depth=5 max_samples=20: first missing fresh sample in round 16 (pool max 21 - depth 5 = 16)

$ NO_OLD=1 ./leak_repro 5 20 30 # control: same run without the old A samples
...
after 3 s: reader got 0 more, newest 65 of 65 written
```

The reader stops receiving B's samples after exactly `max_samples + 1 - depth` old samples from A. The control run never misses one.

With the default limits (history depth 100, `max_samples` 5000) the same thing happens at round 4901:

```
$ ./leak_repro 100 5000 4910
...
round 4900: old A sample + fresh B sample 9900 -> reader got 1, newest 9900
round 4901: old A sample + fresh B sample 9902 -> reader got 0, newest 9900 (fresh sample missing)
...
depth=100 max_samples=5000: first missing fresh sample in round 4901 (pool max 5001 - depth 100 = 4901)
```

### Fast DDS version/commit

Reproduced with 2.14.5 (ros-jazzy-fastrtps 2.14.5-2noble). Traced in an application with 2.14.7 (tag v2.14.7, 010b908). The code is unchanged on 2.14.x, 3.2.x and master.

### Platform/Architecture

Other. Please specify in Additional context section.

### Transport layer

Default configuration, UDPv4 & SHM

### Additional context

Platform: Ubuntu 24.04 x86_64 (the reproducer ran under WSL 2; the application ran in Docker on a native Linux host), ROS 2 Jazzy binaries.

The leak does not depend on the transport. The reproducer is a single participant, so its delivery is intra-process.

Suggested fix: in that branch, report the sample as not stored. Either return `false` with a rejection reason, so the caller releases the change, or release it before returning. The caller must also still mark the sequence number as received or irrelevant in the `WriterProxy`, so reliability does not ask for it again.

<details><summary>leak_repro.cpp</summary>

Build against a ROS 2 Jazzy install: `g++ -std=c++17 -o leak_repro leak_repro.cpp -I/opt/ros/jazzy/include/fastrtps -I/opt/ros/jazzy/include/fastcdr -L/opt/ros/jazzy/lib -lfastrtps -lfastcdr -Wl,-rpath,/opt/ros/jazzy/lib`

```cpp
// Minimal Fast DDS reproducer for a reader change-pool leak in KEEP_LAST history.
//
// One participant, one keyless topic, two reliable writers A and B, and one reliable KEEP_LAST(depth) reader with
// resource_limits.max_samples = M that reads (never takes), so its history stays full.
//   1. B writes `depth` samples stamped now. The reader's instance is full.
//   2. Each round, A writes one sample with write_w_timestamp(now - 10 s). It is older than the instance's front
//      sample, so DataReaderHistory::received_change_keep_last discards it but returns true, and the reader's
//      change header is neither stored nor released.
//      Then B writes one sample stamped now, which should replace the oldest one. The reader reads what is new.
//   3. Once the leaked headers reach M + 1 - depth, the pool has no free header, B's fresh samples are refused, and
//      the reader receives nothing more for the rest of the run.
// usage: [NO_OLD=1] leak_repro [depth=5] [max_samples=20] [rounds=30]
#include <chrono>
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <thread>

#include <fastdds/dds/domain/DomainParticipant.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/domain/DomainParticipantFactory.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/publisher/DataWriter.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/publisher/Publisher.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/subscriber/DataReader.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/subscriber/SampleInfo.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/subscriber/Subscriber.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/topic/Topic.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/topic/TypeSupport.hpp>
#include <fastrtps/types/DynamicDataFactory.h>
#include <fastrtps/types/DynamicPubSubType.h>
#include <fastrtps/types/DynamicTypeBuilderFactory.h>
#include <fastrtps/types/DynamicTypeBuilderPtr.h>
#include <fastrtps/types/DynamicTypePtr.h>

using namespace eprosima::fastdds::dds;
using namespace eprosima::fastrtps::types;
using eprosima::fastrtps::Time_t;

int main(int argc, char ** argv)
{
  const int depth = argc > 1 ? std::atoi(argv[1]) : 5;
  const int max_samples = argc > 2 ? std::atoi(argv[2]) : 20;
  const int rounds = argc > 3 ? std::atoi(argv[3]) : 30;
  const bool no_old = std::getenv("NO_OLD") != nullptr;

  DynamicTypeBuilder_ptr sb = DynamicTypeBuilderFactory::get_instance()->create_struct_builder();
  sb->add_member(0, "v", DynamicTypeBuilderFactory::get_instance()->create_int32_type());
  sb->set_name("Sample");
  DynamicType_ptr dt = sb->build();
  TypeSupport type(new DynamicPubSubType(dt));

  DomainParticipant * p = DomainParticipantFactory::get_instance()->create_participant(
    std::getenv("DOMAIN") ? std::atoi(std::getenv("DOMAIN")) : 91, PARTICIPANT_QOS_DEFAULT);
  type.register_type(p);
  Topic * topic = p->create_topic("leak_repro", type.get_type_name(), TOPIC_QOS_DEFAULT);

  DataWriterQos wq = DATAWRITER_QOS_DEFAULT;
  wq.reliability().kind = RELIABLE_RELIABILITY_QOS;
  wq.history().kind = KEEP_LAST_HISTORY_QOS;
  wq.history().depth = 1000;
  Publisher * pub = p->create_publisher(PUBLISHER_QOS_DEFAULT);
  DataWriter * wa = pub->create_datawriter(topic, wq);
  DataWriter * wb = pub->create_datawriter(topic, wq);

  DataReaderQos rq = DATAREADER_QOS_DEFAULT;
  rq.reliability().kind = RELIABLE_RELIABILITY_QOS;
  rq.history().kind = KEEP_LAST_HISTORY_QOS;
  rq.history().depth = depth;
  rq.resource_limits().max_samples = max_samples;
  rq.resource_limits().allocated_samples = depth;
  Subscriber * sub = p->create_subscriber(SUBSCRIBER_QOS_DEFAULT);
  DataReader * r = sub->create_datareader(topic, rq);

  std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(500));
  DynamicData * d = DynamicDataFactory::get_instance()->create_data(dt);
  DynamicData * in = DynamicDataFactory::get_instance()->create_data(dt);
  auto now = []() {Time_t t; Time_t::now(t); return t;};
  auto settle = []() {std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(20));};
  // read every unread sample, return how many and the newest value
  auto drain = [&](int & newest) {
      int n = 0;
      SampleInfo info;
      while (r->read_next_sample(in, &info) == ReturnCode_t::RETCODE_OK) {
        if (info.valid_data) {
          int32_t x = 0;
          in->get_int32_value(x, 0);
          newest = x;
          ++n;
        }
      }
      return n;
    };

  int v = 0, newest = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < depth; ++i) {
    d->set_int32_value(++v, 0);
    wb->write_w_timestamp(d, HANDLE_NIL, now());
  }
  settle();
  std::printf("fill: B wrote %d, reader got %d\n", depth, drain(newest));

  int stalled_from = -1;
  for (int i = 1; i <= rounds; ++i) {
    Time_t old = now();  // ten seconds before now
    old.seconds -= 10;
    d->set_int32_value(++v, 0);
    if (!no_old) {wa->write_w_timestamp(d, HANDLE_NIL, old);}  // NO_OLD=1: control run without old samples
    settle();
    d->set_int32_value(++v, 0);
    const int fresh = v;
    wb->write_w_timestamp(d, HANDLE_NIL, now());
    settle();
    const int got = drain(newest);
    const bool ok = newest == fresh;
    std::printf("round %2d: old A sample + fresh B sample %d -> reader got %d, newest %d %s\n", i, fresh, got,
      newest, ok ? "" : "(fresh sample missing)");
    if (!ok && stalled_from < 0) {stalled_from = i;}
  }
  std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(3));  // give reliable resends time
  const int late = drain(newest);
  std::printf("after 3 s: reader got %d more, newest %d of %d written\n", late, newest, v);
  std::printf("depth=%d max_samples=%d: first missing fresh sample in round %d (pool max %d - depth %d = %d)\n",
    depth, max_samples, stalled_from, max_samples + 1, depth, max_samples + 1 - depth);

  DynamicDataFactory::get_instance()->delete_data(d);
  DynamicDataFactory::get_instance()->delete_data(in);
  p->delete_contained_entities();
  DomainParticipantFactory::get_instance()->delete_participant(p);
  return stalled_from < 0 ? 0 : 1;
}
```

</details>
